course: dictionary
lesson: 10679
title: Unpack: two meanings, one word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Unpack is a verb, so it is a doing word. Its first meaning is everyday: to take things out of a bag, suitcase or box. «Unpacking your favourite things first helps a new bedroom feel like home.» Here the context clue is bedroom and home, so unpacking means taking your things out of boxes and cases.
explain: The second meaning is about thinking. To unpack something means to explain it step by step so it is easier to understand. Teachers say, «Let's unpack this question», which means: find what it tells you and what it asks.
explain: You can tell which meaning is meant from the words around it. Shopping, basket and suitcase point to the first meaning. Question, problem and idea point to the second. Check by swapping in your meaning: "take the problem out of a box" makes no sense, so it must mean explain.
fact: Unpack means to take things out of a bag, suitcase or box.
fact: Unpack can also mean to explain something step by step so it is easier to understand.
fact: Everyday phrases include unpack the shopping, unpack your bag and unpack the problem.
fact: «Unpacking your favourite things first helps a new bedroom feel like home.»
fact: The verb changes its form: unpack, unpacks, unpacked, unpacking.
exercise: «Unpacking your favourite things first helps a new bedroom feel like home.» What does unpacking mean here? | Cleaning the walls of the bedroom | Packing things up to leave the house | *Taking things out of boxes and cases | Buying new furniture for the room
exercise: Ms Patel says, "Let's unpack this question." What does she want the class to do? | *Find what the question tells us and what it asks | Answer it without reading it | Throw the question away | Put the question in a bag
exercise: Find and copy one word from this sentence that means taking things out: «The Mole begs to unpack the basket by himself, gasping at every packet.» | gasping | basket | Mole | *unpack
test: Dad says, "Unpack your bag before you go out to play." What should you do? | Lock your bag in the car | *Take everything out of your bag | Put more things into your bag | Throw your bag away
test: «Packing the basket was not quite such pleasant work as unpacking the basket.» What does this sentence tell you? | *Unpacking was more pleasant than packing | Packing and unpacking were exactly the same | Packing was more pleasant than unpacking | The basket was always empty
exam: Which phrase uses unpack to mean explain step by step? | Unpack the shopping | Unpack the suitcase | *Unpack the problem | Unpack your bag
